    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The study suggests that motion perception deficits in schizophrenia are not necessarily a direct consequence of eye-tracking abnormalities, as performance was equal when eye movement was minimized (150 msec). Instead, the findings imply that while eye tracking may benefit healthy controls during longer stimulus durations, patients fail to utilize this mechanism effectively. Therefore, the deficit is likely independent of, or at least not strictly caused by, eye-tracking dysfunction.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
      "success": true,
