    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4571",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This question refers to the following information.\nRead the the following quotation to answer questions.\n1848 was the decisive year of German, and so of European, history: it recapitulated Germany's past and inspired Germany's future. … Never has there been a revolution so inspired by a limitless faith in the power of ideas; never has a revolution so discredited the power of ideas in its result. The success of the revolution discredited conservative ideas; the failure of the revolution discredited liberal ideas. After it, nothing remained but the idea of Force, and this idea stood at the helm of German history from then on. For the first time since 1521, the German people stepped on to the centre of the German stage only to miss their cue once more. German history reached its turning-point and failed to turn. This was the fateful essence of 1848.\nA. J. P. Taylor, The Course of German History, 1945\nThe subject of Taylor's analysis in this quotation is\n\nOptions: \nA. the importance of the revolutions of 1848 in modern German history\nB. the shift from conservative to liberal ideas in German history\nC. the relationship between Germany and Europe in the 19th century\nD. the industrial revolution in the context of German history\nE. the influence of the revolutions of 1848 on European history\nF. the role of force in shaping German history\nG. the role of the German people in the revolutions of 1848\nH. the failure of the revolutions of 1848\nI. the impact of the revolutions of 1521 on German history\nJ. the rise of the Nazi Party in Germany\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The quotation focuses on the role and consequences of the 1848 revolutions in German history, emphasizing their decisive nature and ultimate failure. The author analyzes how these revolutions discredited both conservative and liberal ideas, leaving only the idea of force. Thus, the subject is the importance of the revolutions of 1848 in modern German history.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 403,
        "output_tokens": 71,
        "total_tokens": 474
      },
      "provider": "deepseek",
