    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4738",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This question refers to the following information.\n\"One-half of the people of this nation to-day are utterly powerless to blot from the statute books an unjust law, or to write there a new and a just one. The women, dissatisfied as they are with this form of government, that enforces taxation without representation,—that compels them to obey laws to which they have never given their consent,—that imprisons and hangs them without a trial by a jury of their peers, that robs them, in marriage, of the custody of their own persons, wages and children,—are this half of the people left wholly at the mercy of the other half, in direct violation of the spirit and letter of the declarations of the framers of this government, every one of which was based on the immutable principle of equal rights to all.\"\n—Susan B. Anthony, \"I Stand Before You Under Indictment\" (speech), 1873\nWhich of the following statements best represents the criticism of Andrew Carnegie found in this cartoon?\n\nOptions: \nA. Carnegie was dividing his attention and was therefore not as successful in either of his main endeavors.\nB. Carnegie is illustrated as being indifferent to the welfare of his workers.\nC. Carnegie's wealth was unfairly accumulated through exploitation of his workers.\nD. Carnegie's wealth was unjustly accumulated at the expense of his workers' rights.\nE. Carnegie did not give enough of his considerable fortune to charity.\nF. Carnegie was able to give away a great deal of money only because he violated his workers’ rights.\nG. Carnegie's charitable acts were a facade to hide his ruthless business practices.\nH. Carnegie was only charitable for the purpose of enhancing his public image.\nI. Carnegie's ruthless business practices were causing him to lose touch with his working-class origins.\nJ. Carnegie's philanthropy was overshadowed by his poor treatment of employees.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The question asks which statement best represents the criticism of Andrew Carnegie found in the cartoon, but the cartoon itself is not provided in the text. However, based on the speech excerpt from Susan B. Anthony, which criticizes injustice, inequality, and exploitation, the most relevant criticism of Carnegie would involve unfair accumulation of wealth through worker exploitation. Thus, the best choice is likely **C** or **D**, but since both are similar, **C** is the most direct.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 437,
        "output_tokens": 100,
        "total_tokens": 537
      },
