      "generated_answer": "We want positive integers $ n $ such that $ n+2 \\mid 3(n+3)(n^2+9) $.  \nLet $ d = n+2 $, so $ n = d-2 $, and substitute:  \n$$\n3(n+3)(n^2+9) = 3(d+1)((d-2)^2 + 9) = 3(d+1)(d^2 - 4d + 13).\n$$\nWe require $ d \\mid 3(d+1)(d^2 - 4d + 13) $. Since $ \\gcd(d, d+1) = 1 $ and $ \\gcd(d, d^2 - 4d + 13) = \\gcd(d, 13) $, it follows that $ d \\mid 3 \\cdot 13 = 39 $.  \nThus $ d $ is a positive divisor of 39: $ d \\in \\{1, 3, 13, 39\\} $. Corresponding $ n = d - 2 $ gives $ n = -1, 1, 11, 37 $; only positive $ n $ are $ 1, 11, 37 $.  \nCheck: all satisfy the divisibility (e.g., for $ n=1 $, $ n+2=3 $, and $ 3(4)(10)=120 $, divisible by 3).  \nSum: $ 1 + 11 + 37 = \\boxed{49} $.",
