    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine when the greedy algorithm fails for coins of 1, 10, and 25 cents, let $g(N)$ be the number of coins used by the greedy algorithm and $m(N)$ be the minimum number of coins needed.\n1. The greedy algorithm fails if $m(N) < g(N)$. For our denominations, this occurs specifically when $N$ requires several 10-cent coins that could have been better represented by 25-cent coins if the remainder was handled differently.\n2. By checking small values, we find the first failure at $N=30$: greedy uses $25+1+1+1+1+1$ (6 coins), but $10+10+10$ uses 3 coins. Similarly, failures occur for $N=31$ (greedy 7, min 4) up to $N=34$ (greedy 10, min 7), and again at $N=80$ (greedy $25+25+25+1+...$, min $10 \\times 8$).\n3. The greedy algorithm for $\\{1, 10, 25\\}$ fails if and only if the remainder of $N \\pmod{25}$ falls in the range $[30, 49]$ or $[80, 99]$ etc., but since we only have three denominations, the condition for failure is $N \\pmod{25} \\in \\{20, 21, 22, 23, 24\\}$ is false; rather, the specific values where greedy is sub-optimal are $N$ such that $N = 25q + r$. Analysis shows failure occurs if $r \\in \\{5, 6, 7, 8, 9\\}$ and $q \\geq 1$ (e.g., $N=30 \\implies q=1, r=5$), or $r \\in \\{10, 11, 12, 13, 14\\}$ and $q \\geq 2$ (e.g., $N=60 \\implies q=2, r=10$). Specifically, for $1 \\le N \\le 1000$, the greedy algorithm fails if $N \\pmod{25} \\in \\{5, 6, 7, 8, 9\\}$ for $N \\ge 30$ and $N \\pmod{25} \\in \\{10, 11, 12, 13, 14\\}$ for $N \\ge 60$. There are 5 such values in every block of 25 starting from 30, and another 5 in every block starting from 60.\nSumming these up, there are 10 \"failure\" values in every 25 integers starting from $N=50$. In the range 1-1000 (40 blocks of 25), we subtract the cases where $N < 30$ and $N < 60$. Total failures = $40 \\times 10 - 5 (\\text{for } N < 30) - 10 (\\text{for } N < 60) = 400 - 15 = 385$.\nThe number of successful values is $1000 - 185 = 815$. (Correcting calculation: Failures are $N \\in \\{30-34, 55-59, 60-64, 80-84, 85-89, 90-94, \\dots\\}$). The failures are exactly when $N \\pmod{25} \\in \\{5, \\dots, 9\\}$ for $N \\ge 30$ and $N \\pmod{25} \\in \\{10, \\dots, 14\\}$ for $N \\ge 60$. Total failures = $39 \\times 5 - 1$ (for $30-34$) $+ 38 \\times 5 - 2 = 185$.\nTotal successes = $1000 - 185 = 815$.\n\n\\boxed{815}",
