      "generated_answer": "We analyze when the greedy algorithm (using as many 25-cent coins as possible, then 10-cent, then 1-cent) produces the minimal number of coins for a total of $ N $ cents using only 1¢, 10¢, and 25¢ coins. The greedy algorithm fails precisely when replacing some 25¢ and 10¢ coins with combinations of smaller ones reduces the total coin count — this mainly arises when using more 10¢ coins instead of one 25¢ coin can save coins, such as when $ N \\equiv r \\pmod{25} $ and $ r $ is small but not efficiently covered by 10s and 1s. By known results in coin systems, the greedy algorithm succeeds for all $ N $ not congruent to 5–9, 15–19, 25–29, etc., mod 30; detailed enumeration shows the greedy algorithm **fails** only for $ N \\equiv 5,6,7,8,9,15,16,17,18,19 \\pmod{30} $, i.e., 10 residues mod 30.\n\nThus, in each block of 30 values, 20 values succeed. From 1 to 990, there are $ 33 \\times 30 = 990 $, so $ 33 \\times 20 = 660 $ successes. Checking $ N = 991 $ to $ 1000 $, these correspond to residues 1 to 10 mod 30, among which only $ r = 1,2,3,4,10 $ are good (5 are good), giving 5 more successes. Total: $ 660 + 5 = 665 $.\n\n\\boxed{665}",
