      "generated_answer": "To find the value of the product $P = \\prod_{k=0}^6 (\\omega^{3k} + \\omega^k + 1)$, we first note that $\\omega = e^{i 2\\pi/7}$ is a root of $z^7 - 1 = 0$, and the roots of $z^7 - 1 = 0$ are $\\omega^k$ for $k=0, 1, \\dots, 6$. The expression $\\omega^{3k} + \\omega^k + 1$ can be viewed as the polynomial $f(z) = z^3 + z + 1$ evaluated at $z = \\omega^k$.\n\nBy the property of roots of unity, the product of a polynomial $f(z)$ evaluated at all $n$-th roots of unity is given by $\\prod_{k=0}^{n-1} f(\\omega^k) = (-1)^{n(m)} \\text{Res}(z^n - 1, f(z))$, where $m$ is the degree of $f$. Alternatively, if $\\alpha, \\beta, \\gamma$ are the roots of $z^3 + z + 1 = 0$, then:\n\\[ \\prod_{k=0}^6 (\\omega^{3k} + \\omega^k + 1) = \\prod_{k=0}^6 (\\omega^k - \\alpha)(\\omega^k - \\beta)(\\omega^k - \\gamma) = \\prod_{k=0}^6 (\\alpha - \\omega^k) \\prod_{k=0}^6 (\\beta - \\omega^k) \\prod_{k=0}^6 (\\gamma - \\omega^k) \\]\nSince $\\prod_{k=0}^6 (x - \\omega^k) = x^7 - 1$, the product is $(\\alpha^7 - 1)(\\beta^7 - 1)(\\gamma^7 - 1)$.\n\nTo find $(\\alpha^7 - 1)(\\beta^7 - 1)(\\gamma^7 - 1) = (\\alpha\\beta\\gamma)^7 - (\\alpha^7\\beta^7 + \\beta^7\\gamma^7 + \\gamma^7\\alpha^7) + (\\alpha^7 + \\beta^7 + \\gamma^7) - 1$, we use the power sums $S_n$ for $z^3 + z + 1 = 0$:\n$S_1 = 0, S_2 = -2, S_3 = -3, S_4 = 2, S_5 = 5, S_6 = -1, S_7 = -7$.\nUsing the property that $\\alpha, \\beta, \\gamma$ are roots of $z^3 + z + 1 = 0$, we have $\\alpha^3 = -\\alpha - 1$. Calculating $\\alpha^7 = \\alpha(\\alpha^3)^2 = \\alpha(-\\alpha-1)^2 = \\alpha^3 + 2\\alpha^2 + \\alpha = 2\\alpha^2 - 1$.\nThus, $\\prod (\\alpha^7 - 1) = \\prod (2\\alpha^2 - 2) = 8 \\prod (\\alpha-1) \\prod (\\alpha+1)$.\nSince $f(1) = 3$ and $f(-1) = -1$, the product is $8 (-f(1)) (f(-1)) = 8(-3)(-1) = 24$.\n\n\\boxed{24}",
