      "generated_answer": "To evaluate the product  \n$$\n\\prod_{k=0}^6 \\left( \\omega^{3k} + \\omega^k + 1 \\right),\n$$  \nwhere $\\omega = \\cos\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{7}\\right) + i\\sin\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{7}\\right) = e^{i2\\pi/7}$, we recognize that $\\omega$ is a **primitive 7th root of unity**, satisfying $\\omega^7 = 1$ and $\\omega^k$ for $k = 0, 1, \\dots, 6$ are all the 7th roots of unity.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Express the Product in Terms of Roots of Unity\n\nLet us define $x = \\omega^k$, so $\\omega^{3k} = (\\omega^k)^3 = x^3$. Therefore, each term becomes:\n$$\n\\omega^{3k} + \\omega^k + 1 = x^3 + x + 1.\n$$\nSince $\\omega^k$ runs through all 7th roots of unity as $k$ runs from 0 to 6, the product becomes:\n$$\n\\prod_{k=0}^6 \\left( \\omega^{3k} + \\omega^k + 1 \\right) = \\prod_{x^7=1} (x^3 + x + 1).\n$$\nThis is the product of the polynomial $f(x) = x^3 + x + 1$ evaluated at all the 7th roots of unity.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Use Resultant or Product Over Roots of Unity\n\nThe value of the product $\\prod_{x^7=1} f(x)$ is equal to the **resultant** of $f(x)$ and $x^7 - 1$. Alternatively, since $f(x)$ is a cubic polynomial with roots $a_1, a_2, a_3$, we can write:\n$$\n\\prod_{x^7=1} f(x) = \\prod_{i=1}^3 \\prod_{x^7=1} (x - a_i) = \\prod_{i=1}^3 \\left( \\prod_{x^7=1} (x - a_i) \\right).\n$$\nUsing a known identity, for any complex number $a$, the product over all 7th roots of unity of $(x - a)$ is:\n$$\n\\prod_{x^7=1} (x - a) = a^7 - 1.\n$$\nThus:\n$$\n\\prod_{x^7=1} f(x) = \\prod_{i=1}^3 (a_i^7 - 1).\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Compute $a_i^7 - 1$ Using Recurrence\n\nSince each $a_i$ is a root of $f(x) = x^3 + x + 1$, we have:\n$$\na_i^3 = -a_i - 1.\n$$\nFrom this, we can derive a recurrence relation for $a_i^n$ and compute $a_i^7$ in terms of lower powers. After simplification, we find:\n$$\na_i^7 = 2a_i^2 - 1.\n$$\nTherefore:\n$$\na_i^7 - 1 = 2a_i^2 - 2 = 2(a_i^2 - 1).\n$$\nSo:\n$$\n\\prod_{i=1}^3 (a_i^7 - 1) = \\prod_{i=1}^3 2(a_i^2 - 1) = 8 \\prod_{i=1}^3 (a_i^2 - 1).\n$$\n\nNow, note that:\n$$\n\\prod_{i=1}^3 (a_i^2 - 1) = \\prod_{i=1}^3 (a_i - 1)(a_i + 1) = \\left( \\prod_{i=1}^3 (a_i - 1) \\right) \\left( \\prod_{i=1}^3 (a_i + 1) \\right).\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $f(x) = (x - a_1)(x - a_2)(x - a_3)$, we have:\n- $\\prod_{i=1}^3 (a_i - 1) = -f(1) = -3$,\n- $\\prod_{i=1}^3 (a_i + 1) = -f(-1) = 1$.\n\nThus:\n$$\n\\prod_{i=1}^3 (a_i^2 - 1) = (-3)(1) = -3,\n$$\nand:\n$$\n\\prod_{i=1}^3 (a_i^7 - 1) = 8(-3) = -24.\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n$$\n\\prod_{x^7=1} f(x) = -(-24) = 24.\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer\n\n$$\n\\boxed{24}\n$$",
