    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_105",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a 0-indexed array nums of non-negative integers, and two integers l and r.\nReturn the count of sub-multisets within nums where the sum of elements in each subset falls within the inclusive range of [l, r].\nSince the answer may be large, return it modulo 10^9 + 7.\nA sub-multiset is an unordered collection of elements of the array in which a given value x can occur 0, 1, ..., occ[x] times, where occ[x] is the number of occurrences of x in the array.\nNote that:\n\nTwo sub-multisets are the same if sorting both sub-multisets results in identical multisets.\nThe sum of an empty multiset is 0.\n\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: nums = [1,2,2,3], l = 6, r = 6\nOutput: 1\nExplanation: The only subset of nums that has a sum of 6 is {{1, 2, 3}}.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: nums = [2,1,4,2,7], l = 1, r = 5\nOutput: 7\nExplanation: The subsets of nums that have a sum within the range [1, 5] are {{1}}, {{2}}, {{4}}, {{2, 2}}, {{1, 2}}, {{1, 4}}, and {{1, 2, 2}}.\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput: nums = [1,2,1,3,5,2], l = 3, r = 5\nOutput: 9\nExplanation: The subsets of nums that have a sum within the range [3, 5] are {{3}}, {{5}}, {{1, 2}}, {{1, 3}}, {{2, 2}}, {{2, 3}}, {{1, 1, 2}}, {{1, 1, 3}}, and {{1, 2, 2}}.\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= nums.length <= 2 * 10^4\n0 <= nums[i] <= 2 * 10^4\nSum of nums does not exceed 2 * 10^4.\n0 <= l <= r <= 2 * 10^4",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\n\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef countSubMultisets(nums, l, r):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    # Count occurrences of each value\n    freq = Counter(nums)\n    # dp[s] = number of ways to obtain sum s using processed values\n    dp = [0] * (r + 1)\n    dp[0] = 1  # empty multiset\n\n    # Process each distinct number\n    for x, cnt in freq.items():\n        if x == 0:\n            # zero can be taken any number of times (0..cnt) without changing sum\n            # So each existing dp[s] gets multiplied by (cnt+1) ways\n            multiplier = cnt + 1\n            for s in range(r + 1):\n                dp[s] = (dp[s] * multiplier) % MOD\n            continue\n\n        # For non‑zero x, we use the \"bounded knapsack\" trick with sliding window\n        # Create a new dp array to accumulate contributions\n        new_dp = dp[:]  # ways that take zero copies of x\n        # For each remainder modulo x, process using a queue\n        for rem in range(x):\n            queue_sum = 0  # sum of dp values in the window (mod MOD)\n            # The indices we consider: rem, rem+x, rem+2x, ...\n            # We'll slide a window of size (cnt+1)\n            positions = list(range(rem, r + 1, x))\n            # First, fill the initial window and compute queue_sum\n            for i, pos in enumerate(positions):\n                # add dp[pos] to the queue\n                queue_sum = (queue_sum + dp[pos]) % MOD\n                # remove elements that fall out of the window (more than cnt positions behind)\n                if i > cnt:\n                    # the element that falls out is at position positions[i - cnt - 1]\n                    queue_sum = (queue_sum - dp[positions[i - cnt - 1]]) % MOD\n                # new_dp[pos] should also include contributions from taking 1..cnt copies of x\n                # The queue_sum now holds dp[pos] + dp[pos-x] + ... + dp[pos - cnt*x]\n                # But dp[pos] is already counted in new_dp (zero copies), so we subtract it once\n                new_dp[pos] = (new_dp[pos] + queue_sum - dp[pos]) % MOD\n            # After processing this remainder, dp becomes new_dp for the next iteration\n        dp = new_dp\n\n    # Sum over the range [l, r]\n    ans = sum(dp[l:r+1]) % MOD\n    return ans\n\n```",
