    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Here is the well-documented and structured Python function that solves the problem of finding the **minimum number of deletion operations** required to make three given strings equal by repeatedly deleting the last character of any string with length at least 2.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ **Problem Summary**\n\nGiven three strings `s1`, `s2`, and `s3`, we can perform the operation of deleting the last character of any string (if its length is at least 2) as many times as needed.\n\nWe aim to make all three strings equal. The **minimum number of operations** required to achieve this is to be returned. If it is **not possible** to make the strings equal, we return `-1`.\n\n---\n\n### 🧠 **Key Observations**\n\n- We can only delete characters from the **end** of a string.\n- Therefore, the final equal string must be a **common prefix** of all three strings.\n- The **longest such prefix** that all three strings share is the key to minimizing the number of deletions.\n- For each string, the number of deletions required is `len(s_i) - L`, where `L` is the length of the common prefix.\n- If no such common prefix of length at least 1 exists, it is **impossible** to make the strings equal.\n\n---\n\n### 🧮 **Algorithm Steps**\n\n1. **Determine the minimum length** among the three strings.\n2. **Iterate over each character** from index 0 up to the minimum length.\n3. **Compare the characters** at the same index in all three strings.\n4. **Stop** when a mismatch is found or when all characters have been matched.\n5. If the **common prefix length `L` is 0**, return `-1`.\n6. Otherwise, compute and return the **total number of deletions** required to make all strings equal to the common prefix of length `L`.\n\n---\n\n### 🧑‍💻 **Python Implementation**\n\n```python\ndef min_operations(s1: str, s2: str, s3: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the minimum number of deletion operations required\n    to make three strings equal by removing the last character of any string.\n\n    Parameters:\n    - s1, s2, s3: Input strings of lengths between 1 and 100.\n\n    Returns:\n    - int: Minimum number of deletion operations, or -1 if not possible.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Step 1: Determine the minimum length of the three strings\n    min_len = min(len(s1), len(s2), len(s3))\n    \n    # Step 2: Find the longest common prefix length (L)\n    L = 0\n    while L < min_len and s1[L] == s2[L] == s3[L]:\n        L += 1\n\n    # Step 3: If no common prefix of at least length 1 exists\n    if L == 0:\n        return -1\n\n    # Step 4: Compute the total number of deletions required\n    return (len(s1) - L) + (len(s2) - L) + (len(s3) - L)\n```\n\n---\n\n### 📌 **Time and Space Complexity**\n\n- **Time Complexity:** `O(min(len(s1), len(s2), len(s3)))` — since we only compare characters up to the shortest string.\n- **Space Complexity:** `O(1)` — no extra space used.\n\n---\n\nThis function is efficient and handles all edge cases within the given constraints. It ensures that the result is accurate and minimal, returning `-1` only when the strings cannot be made equal.",
