      "generated_answer": "We are given an initial undirected graph G (which may have self-loops and multi-edges) that is \"good\", meaning for K given pairs (x_i, y_i), there is no path between x_i and y_i.\n\nThen we get Q queries, each adding an edge (p_i, q_i). For each query, we need to check if after adding that edge, the graph remains good — i.e., for all j, x_j and y_j remain disconnected.\n\nObservation:\n- Since originally G is good, for each forbidden pair (x_j, y_j), they are in different connected components.\n- Adding an edge (p, q) may connect two previously disconnected components. Specifically, if after adding the edge, some forbidden pair (x_j, y_j) becomes connected, then the answer is \"No\".\n\nWhen does adding edge (p, q) cause x and y to become connected?\n- In the original graph, x and y are in different components.\n- After adding (p, q), if p and q were in components that, when merged, cause x and y to end up in the same component, then x and y become connected.\n\nMore precisely:\nLet the connected components of G be known. Adding edge (p, q) merges the components containing p and q (if they are different). So x and y become connected after the addition iff:\n- In the original graph, x and y are in different components (true by problem statement), AND\n- After merging the components of p and q, x and y end up in the same component.\n\nThis happens iff:\n- x is in the component of p, and y is in the component of q, OR\n- x is in the component of q, and y is in the component of p, OR\n- x is in the merged component (i.e., in comp(p) ∪ comp(q)) and y is also in comp(p) ∪ comp(q), and originally they were in different components.\n\nBut note: originally x and y are in different components. After adding (p,q), the only way x and y become connected is if x and y end up in the same merged component — i.e., x ∈ C1, y ∈ C2, and we merge C1 and C2 via (p,q), meaning p ∈ C1 and q ∈ C2 (or vice versa).\n\nSo: for a forbidden pair (x, y), adding (p, q) causes them to become connected iff:\n- x and y are in different components originally (true), AND\n- The edge (p, q) connects the component of x to the component of y.\n\nThat is: if comp(x) = comp(p) and comp(y) = comp(q), or comp(x) = comp(q) and comp(y) = comp(p). But since comp is symmetric, this is equivalent to:\n- {comp(x), comp(y)} = {comp(p), comp(q)}.\n\nWait — is that sufficient? Let's test:\n\nSuppose comp(x) = A, comp(y) = B, A ≠ B.\nAdding edge (p,q): if p ∈ A, q ∈ B, then after adding, A and B merge, so x and y become connected.\n\nSimilarly, if p ∈ B, q ∈ A, same thing.\n\nSo yes: the condition is that comp(p) and comp(q) are exactly the two components containing x and y (in either order).\n\nBut note: what if there are more than two components involved? Actually, only two components get merged: comp(p) and comp(q). So x and y become connected iff x ∈ comp(p) and y ∈ comp(q), or x ∈ comp(q) and y ∈ comp(p). That is: \ncomp(x) ∈ {comp(p), comp(q)} AND comp(y) ∈ {comp(p), comp(q)} AND comp(x) ≠ comp(y) (which is always true originally).\n\nBut since comp(x) ≠ comp(y) originally, the condition reduces to:\ncomp(x) and comp(y) are both in {comp(p), comp(q)}.\n\nWhich is equivalent to: \n{comp(x), comp(y)} ⊆ {comp(p), comp(q)} and |{comp(x), comp(y)}| = 2.\n\nSince comp(x) ≠ comp(y), this is just:\n{comp(x), comp(y)} = {comp(p), comp(q)}.\n\nSo for each query (p, q), we need to check: does there exist any forbidden pair (x_i, y_i) such that \n{comp(x_i), comp(y_i)} = {comp(p), comp(q)}?\n\nBut note: it's possible that comp(p) == comp(q) (i.e., p and q are already in the same component). Then adding the edge does nothing (or creates a self-loop or multi-edge), so no new connections — thus the graph remains good. So answer \"Yes\" in that case.\n\nHowever, wait: what if comp(p) == comp(q)? Then adding (p,q) doesn't change connectivity. Since original graph was good, it remains good → \"Yes\".\n\nSo:\n- If comp(p) == comp(q): answer \"Yes\".\n- Else: let A = comp(p), B = comp(q). Then if there exists any forbidden pair (x_i, y_i) such that comp(x_i) = A and comp(y_i) = B (or vice versa), then adding (p,q) connects A and B, hence connects x_i and y_i → answer \"No\". Otherwise, \"Yes\".\n\nBut note: there might be multiple forbidden pairs. We need to check if any forbidden pair has components (A,B).\n\nSo plan:\n1. Build graph, ignore self-loops and multi-edges for connectivity (since self-loops don't help connectivity, and multi-edges are redundant).\n2. Run BFS/DFS (but N up to 200,000, so use efficient union-find or BFS with adjacency list) to get connected components. Assign each node a component id (e.g., root or an index).\n3. Preprocess: for each forbidden pair (x_i, y_i), get (c1, c2) = (comp[x_i], comp[y_i]) — and since comp[x_i] != comp[y_i], we can represent the pair as (min(c1,c2), max(c1,c2)) to normalize.\n4. Store all such normalized component pairs in a set (or use a hash set).\n5. For each query (p, q):\n   - Get comp_p = comp[p], comp_q = comp[q].\n   - If comp_p == comp_q: print \"Yes\"\n   - Else: let key = (min(comp_p, comp_q), max(comp_p, comp_q))\n        if key in the set of forbidden component-pairs: print \"No\"\n        else: print \"Yes\"\n\nBut wait: what if there are multiple forbidden pairs that map to the same component pair? Then we only need to know if at least one exists. So a set is perfect.\n\nHowever, note the constraint: \"i ≠ j ⇒ {x_i, y_i} ≠ {x_j, y_j}\" — but note: it's possible that two different forbidden pairs (x_i,y_i) and (x_j,y_j) end up in the same pair of components? Yes, for example: \n  comp(x1)=A, comp(y1)=B; comp(x2)=A, comp(y2)=B — even though (x1,y1) ≠ (x2,y2), their component pairs are the same.\n\nBut the problem doesn't forbid that. So we must consider: if at least one forbidden pair has components (A,B), then adding an edge that connects A and B breaks the condition.\n\nTherefore, we only need to store the set of component-pairs that are forbidden.\n\nBut note: what if a forbidden pair has comp(x_i) = comp(y_i)? That cannot happen because the original graph is good — so there is no path, meaning they are in different components. So comp(x_i) != comp(y_i) for all i.\n\nSo steps:\n\n1. Read N, M.\n2. Build graph: for each edge (u, v), if u != v, add both directions (if u==v, skip for connectivity, but we can ignore self-loops).\n3. Use union-find or BFS to get components. Since N and M up to 200,000, BFS/DFS is fine (but union-find also works). However, union-find is simpler for undirected connectivity without needing to store the entire graph? Actually, we have to build the graph anyway.\n\nBut note: multi-edges: we can use union-find to avoid building adjacency list? Actually, union-find is efficient for M up to 200,000.\n\nLet's use union-find (DSU) for simplicity.\n\nSteps for DSU:\n- Initialize parent[1..N] = i, rank or size for union by rank.\n- For each edge (u, v):\n   if u != v: union(u, v)\n- Then for each node, find its root.\n\nBut note: self-loops (u==v) can be skipped.\n\n4. Read K, then for each forbidden pair (x, y):\n   - r1 = find(x), r2 = find(y). Since the graph is good, r1 != r2.\n   - Let key = (min(r1, r2), max(r1, r2))\n   - Add key to a set `forbidden_comp_pairs`.\n\n5. Read Q, for each query (p, q):\n   - r1 = find(p), r2 = find(q)\n   - if r1 == r2: print \"Yes\"\n   - else: key = (min(r1, r2",
