  - For each monster event, we must have inventory[x] >= 1\n    #   - When we encounter a potion event for type x, we can choose to pick it up only if we need it for a future monster\n    #   - To minimize max inventory, we should delay picking up potions as much as possible\n    \n    # Algorithm:\n    #   1. Precompute for each type x, the list of monster event indices (positions in events list)\n    #   2. For each type x, we know we need exactly monster_count[x] potions before the last monster of type x\n    #   3. We can use a \"demand\" array: for each event index, how many potions of each type are needed up to that point\n    #   4. Actually, we can process events in order and for each potion event, decide if we need it based on remaining monsters\n    \n    # Let's do:\n    #   - For each type x, compute the number of monsters of type x that occur after each position\n    #   - Let need_after[i][x] = number of monsters of type x after event i\n    #   - But we can just keep a running count\n    \n    # Instead, here's a simpler method:\n    #   - Count total monsters per type: need_count[x] = monster_count[x]\n    #   - For each potion event for type x, if we don't have enough potions to cover the monsters that will come after this point, we need to pick it up\n    #   - But how many monsters of type x will come after this point? We can precompute suffix counts\n    \n    # Precompute suffix monster counts:\n    suffix_monster_count = defaultdict(int)\n    # We'll compute for each event index, the remaining monsters for each type\n    # But storing full dict for each index is O(N^2), too expensive\n    \n    # Instead, we can do:\n    #   - First pass: count monsters per type\n    #   - Second pass: for each event, decrement the monster count when we see a monster\n    #   - This gives us for each event, the remaining monsters for each type after that event\n    \n    remaining_monsters = defaultdict(int)\n    for t, x in events:\n        if t == 2:\n            remaining_monsters[x] += 1\n    \n    # Now simulate:\n    inventory = defaultdict(int)  # current inventory per type\n    max_inventory = 0\n    actions = []  # actions for potion events only\n    \n    # For each potion event, we'll decide whether to pick it up\n    # We'll use a strategy: only pick up a potion if we will need it to defeat a monster before the next opportunity to get a potion of the same type\n    \n    # Let's precompute for each type x, the list of potion event indices and monster event indices\n    potion_events = defaultdict(list)  # type -> list of indices\n    monster_events = defaultdict(list)  # type -> list of indices\n    \n    for i, (t, x) in enumerate(events):\n        if t == 1:\n            potion_events[x].append(i)\n        else:\n            monster_events[x].append(i)\n    \n    # For each type x, for each monster event, we need to have enough potions picked up before it\n    # Let's define a \"deficit\" for each type: how many more potions we need before each monster event\n    \n    # We can process events in order and maintain for each type:\n    #   - picked[x] = number of potions of type x picked up so far\n    #   - when we see a monster event for type x, we must have picked[x] - (monsters_defeated[x] so far) >= 1\n    #     but monsters_defeated[x] = number of monster events of type x we've processed so far\n    \n    # Actually, simpler: maintain a counter for each type: needed = remaining monsters of that type\n    # When we see a potion event for type x, if needed[x] > current_inventory[x], we should pick it up\n    # But that's not quite right: we might have picked up extra potions that we don't need\n    \n    # Better: we know the total number of monsters per type. Let's say for type x, we need exactly need_count[x] potions.\n    # We should pick up a potion only when we don't have enough to cover the remaining monsters of that type.\n    \n    # Specifically, let's maintain:\n    #   - remaining_monsters[x]: h